<body>
  <GameNav />
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Rooms &amp; Exits</a> &gt; Chapter 3 &gt; Level 7</nav>

  <main class="seo-section">
    <h1>Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 3 Level 7 Walkthrough: Master Bathroom</h1>

    <p>Master Bathroom is the seventh room of Chapter 3, <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and it is the first room in the chapter that gives you somewhere to walk. The camera pans across a double vanity, a freestanding tub and a glass shower, and there is a separate toilet alcove behind a partition with its own towel rack. Two keypads carry the level: a six-dial panel on the toilet cistern and a four-digit lock on the door. This page gives you both codes, the objects they come from, and a plain account of the two puzzles in this room whose answers we could not read off the footage.</p>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Webelinx Games, the developer of Rooms &amp; Exits: Escape Room Games.</div>

    <div class="verify-note">Rebuilt frame by frame from a single 3:15 recording, sampled at one frame per second, with the cistern dial re-sampled at eight frames per second where the digits were still moving. <b>279151</b> was read from the dial at 1:33 and again from the last frame before the panel closed, and the three tile values behind it (27, 91, 51) were read separately. <b>6389</b> was read from the shower tiles at 3:05 and again from the keypad display at 3:09. Every digit here comes off the pixels; nothing is copied from another guide.</div>

    <LevelSelector chapter={3} current={7} variant="jump" label="Chapter 3 · Murder Dressed in White" />

    <div class="answer-box">
      <span class="label">Quick answer</span>
      <p>Master Bathroom is room 7 of 15 in Chapter 3. Two keypads, two codes: <b>279151</b> on the six-dial panel built into the toilet cistern, and <b>6389</b> on the door keypad.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Open the toilet alcove and look at the cistern lid: three tiles sit in shaped slots — <b>27</b> in a parallelogram, <b>16</b> turned upside down as <b>91</b> in the oval, and <b>51</b> in the rhombus.</li>
        <li>The six buttons on the front of the cistern read that left-to-right run: <b>2 7 9 1 5 1</b>. The lid pops open and there is an item floating in the water.</li>
        <li>Look at the shower's beige wall tiles, right of the handheld sprayer, for four pale digits: <b>6 3 8 9</b>.</li>
        <li>Type <b>6389</b> into the black door keypad — red display, yellow keys — and confirm with the arrow. The door swings ajar and the exit icon appears.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="video-wrap">
      <div class="yt-facade" data-yt="lKaQv2AZ6Ps" data-title="Rooms &amp; Exits Level 7 Master Bathroom Walkthrough Murder Dressed in White Chapter"
           role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Play video: Rooms &amp; Exits Level 7 Master Bathroom Walkthrough">
        <img src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/lKaQv2AZ6Ps/maxresdefault.jpg"
             alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 7 Master Bathroom walkthrough video" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1280" height="720"
             onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src='https://i.ytimg.com/vi/lKaQv2AZ6Ps/hqdefault.jpg'">
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="video-cap">The 3:15 run used for every frame on this page.</p>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l7-hero-master-bathroom.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 7 Master Bathroom: a double vanity with two mirrors on the left, a wooden door in the centre, a freestanding tub with a gold rim, and a glass walk-in shower on the right" width="1200" height="675" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The room is one long wall and one open floor. On the left, a tall white open shelf stands against the wall, then the double vanity: a sage-green cabinet run with two white basins, gold taps, two rectangular mirrors above and drawers below. In the middle stands a panelled wooden door with a black keypad on it. To its right the freestanding tub sits under a multi-bulb brass chandelier, with two recessed niches in the tiled wall above holding bottles, and a two-tier wooden side table beside it carrying folded towels and two candles. On the far right is the walk-in shower, glass panels, a bench of green mosaic tiles and beige brick-laid wall tiles. A square metal floor drain sits in the tiles between the tub and the side table.</p>
    <p>Behind a partition, off the main room, is the toilet alcove: a modern white toilet, a wall-mounted brass toilet-paper holder with a patterned roll, a gold-accented brush holder, and a chrome towel rack. This alcove is where the level's main lock lives, which is why the recording spends a full minute there and almost none in the shower.</p>
    <p>The level is two keypads and a set of clue props between them. The cistern panel is the one that needs the room read first, because its six digits come from three tiles lying on the lid. The door keypad is the one you can solve the moment you spot the shower tiles. Everything else — the drawer lights, the towel order, the drain, the cube — is either a sub-puzzle with no number attached or a container for an item, and this page says which is which.</p>

    <h2>The map: what is where<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Every interactive object we could identify, and what it is for.</p>
    <table class="item-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Where</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Vanity drawers and mirrors</td><td>Left wall, under the two mirrors</td><td>Light pattern puzzle; banner at 0:24 reads "Arrange the light on the drawer like that on the mirror".</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tall white shelf</td><td>Far left, beside the vanity</td><td>Open shelves; the cabinet below opens for a look.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Floor drain</td><td>Tiles between tub and side table</td><td>Square metal drain, a hotspot at 0:42; likely where a tool is used.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bathtub</td><td>Centre right, under the chandelier</td><td>Fills with water during the run; two niches above it hold bottles.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Side table</td><td>Right of the tub</td><td>Two tiers: folded towels and two candles.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shower wall tiles</td><td>Far right, behind the glass</td><td>Four pale digits set into the tiles: <b>6 3 8 9</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Toilet cistern top</td><td>Toilet alcove, behind the partition</td><td>Three shaped slots holding numbered tiles: parallelogram <b>27</b>, oval <b>16/91</b>, rhombus <b>51</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Six-dial panel</td><td>Front of the cistern</td><td>Six oval buttons, each 0-9. Code <b>279151</b>; opens the lid.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Towel rack</td><td>Toilet alcove wall</td><td>Folded grey towels; banner at 1:04 reads "Order of the towels".</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Toilet-paper roll</td><td>Brass holder beside the toilet</td><td>Patterned paper — a grid and stars — that reads as a clue rather than a container.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cube</td><td>Floating centre-screen at 2:16</td><td>Yellow and maroon block cube; banner at 2:25 reads "Touch order for the cube".</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Door keypad</td><td>On the wooden door</td><td>Black panel, red display, yellow 1-9 keys, X and enter. Code <b>6389</b>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The order below follows the recording. Timestamps are video time; the links jump to the matching second.</p>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:04</span>
      <strong>Walk the room before you touch a drawer</strong>
      The level opens on the vanity wall and pulls back to the full room within a few seconds. Use those seconds to fix the geography, because this room is bigger than anything earlier in Chapter 3 and the two keypads are at opposite ends of it. Left to right: tall white shelf, double vanity with two mirrors, wooden door with a black keypad, freestanding tub under the brass chandelier with two niches above it, side table with towels and candles, glass shower with beige brick tiles. The toilet is not in this view at all — it sits in its own alcove behind the partition, and that is where the main lock is.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l7-hero-master-bathroom.jpg" alt="The Master Bathroom at the start of Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 7: double vanity and mirrors on the left, wooden door in the centre, freestanding tub, glass shower on the right" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lKaQv2AZ6Ps&t=4s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:04</a></div>
    </div>
    <InContentAd />

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:24 · vanity</span>
      <strong>The drawer-light puzzle states its own rule</strong>
      Tap the vanity drawers and the game prints the instruction across the top of the screen: <b>Arrange the light on the drawer like that on the mirror</b>. The target is the reflection in the two mirrors above the basins; the thing you move is the lit pattern on the drawer fronts below. This is a matching puzzle, not a code — no digits appear anywhere in it in the footage. The recording works through it and moves on, which is why this page treats it as a step you must clear rather than a number you can copy.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l7-vanity-drawer-light.jpg" alt="The vanity in Master Bathroom with the instruction banner Arrange the light on the drawer like that on the mirror" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lKaQv2AZ6Ps&t=24s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:24</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 0:30 · the first number</span>
      <strong>A tile reading 16 drops into your bag</strong>
      Six seconds after the drawer instruction, the screen dims and a large oval overlay appears carrying the number <b>16</b>. That overlay is the game's "you picked up a numbered tile" beat: the same tile turns up later in the middle slot of the cistern lid. Remember the value as <b>16</b>, because when you meet it again it will be lying the other way up and read <b>91</b> — the same physical object, rotated. If you only remember one number for the whole room, remember this one.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l7-number-16-popup.jpg" alt="A translucent oval overlay showing the number 16 picked up in Master Bathroom" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lKaQv2AZ6Ps&t=30s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:30</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 0:42 · the drain</span>
      <strong>The square floor drain is a hotspot, not a clue</strong>
      The camera drops to the tiles between the tub and the side table and centres on a square metal drain with a slotted cover. Nothing is readable on it — no number, no symbol, no hint — but the player goes to it immediately after opening the vanity cabinets and the inventory, and the next thing the camera shows is the drain again. That is enough to call it a tool target: check it with whatever you are carrying, and expect the reward to be an item rather than a code. We never see the drain open in a single clean shot, so treat the order as probable.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l7-floor-drain.jpg" alt="The square metal floor drain in the tiles between the bathtub and the side table in Master Bathroom" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lKaQv2AZ6Ps&t=42s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:42</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 1:04 · toilet alcove</span>
      <strong>"Order of the towels"</strong>
      The camera moves into the toilet alcove and a second banner appears: <b>Order of the towels</b>. The chrome rack on the wall carries several folded grey towels, and the game wants them in a set sequence. This is a second matching-style puzzle with no digits attached, and the recording solves it without ever holding a frame long enough to read the target order off the towels. It is listed under "What this page could not verify" rather than guessed at.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l7-towel-order.jpg" alt="The towel rack in the toilet alcove of Master Bathroom with the instruction Order of the towels" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lKaQv2AZ6Ps&t=64s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:04</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 1:22 · the cistern lid</span>
      <strong>Three tiles, three shapes: 27, 16/91, 51</strong>
      The top of the cistern is a puzzle in itself. It has three shaped recesses — a parallelogram on the left, an oval in the middle with a curved arrow above it, and a rhombus on the right — and by 1:22 they hold three numbered tiles: <b>27</b> on the left, <b>16</b> in the middle, <b>51</b> on the right. Read left to right that is <b>27 16 51</b>, six digits. The middle tile is the catch: the arrow above it means it rotates, and rotated 180 degrees the same tile reads <b>91</b>, so the row becomes <b>27 91 51</b>. That is the number the panel in front of you wants.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l7-cistern-slots.jpg" alt="The toilet cistern top in Master Bathroom with three slots holding the numbered tiles 27, 91 and 51" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lKaQv2AZ6Ps&t=82s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:22</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 7 · 1:33 · the code</span>
      <strong>Set the six buttons to 279151</strong>
      The front of the cistern carries six oval buttons in a row, each cycling 0-9 when tapped. In the recording they start at <b>0 0 0 0 0 0</b> at 1:24 and climb — 2 3 0 0 0 0, then 2 7 0 0 0 0, then 2 7 9 0 0 0 — until the row reads <b>2 7 9 1 5 1</b>. Those are exactly the three tile values, two digits each, in slot order: <b>27</b>, <b>91</b>, <b>51</b>. The sixth digit is the one that matters most: the row sits at 279150 for about half a second and the lid only releases once the last button is tapped round to <b>1</b>.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l7-cistern-keypad-279151.jpg" alt="The six-button panel on the toilet cistern in Master Bathroom set to 279151" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lKaQv2AZ6Ps&t=93s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:33</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 8 · 1:34 · the lid opens</span>
      <strong>What 279151 buys you</strong>
      The panel closes, the cistern lid swings up, and there is something floating in the water — a pale rectangular object, roughly tile-sized, with a texture on it. The recording holds on it and then moves on. This is the payoff for the whole tile-and-dial chain, and it is worth taking the extra second to look at it rather than tapping through: the object is a clue for something later in the room, not an exit key.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l7-cistern-open.jpg" alt="The toilet cistern in Master Bathroom opened after 279151, with an object floating in the water" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lKaQv2AZ6Ps&t=94s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:34</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 9 · 2:16 · the cube</span>
      <strong>A yellow-and-maroon cube arrives centre-screen</strong>
      After the cistern, the camera cuts away from the bathroom to a floating object: a cube built from small yellow and dark-red blocks, shown with a corner toward you. It is not sitting on any surface in the room — it is a puzzle overlay, the game's way of handing you something to manipulate rather than something to find. The cube is the reason the toilet-paper roll's grid pattern is worth a second look: a 3x3 grid and a block cube are the same shape of clue.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l7-cube-puzzle.jpg" alt="The yellow and dark red cube puzzle in Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 7 Master Bathroom" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lKaQv2AZ6Ps&t=136s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:16</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 10 · 2:25 · touch order</span>
      <strong>"Touch order for the cube"</strong>
      The banner over the cube reads <b>Touch order for the cube</b>, which tells you the puzzle is a sequence of taps on its faces, not a number. Around 2:34 the same pattern is shown flattened as a 3x3 grid of yellow, green and maroon triangles — the cube unrolled. This page does not print a tap order for it: at one frame per second we can see the grid but not the order the player touches it in, and an invented sequence would waste more of your time than it saves.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l7-cube-touch-order.jpg" alt="The banner Touch order for the cube over the cistern area in Master Bathroom" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lKaQv2AZ6Ps&t=145s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:25</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 11 · 3:05 · the second code</span>
      <strong>6389 is printed on the shower tiles</strong>
      Zoom into the shower wall and four pale digits are set into the beige brick tiles, to the right of the handheld sprayer and its hose: <b>6 3 8 9</b>. They are not written in grime or reflection — they are part of the tiling, which is why they are easy to miss: same tone as the grout, in the one corner of the room you have no other reason to inspect. Once you have them, you have the whole second half of the level.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l7-shower-tiles-6389.jpg" alt="The shower wall tiles in Master Bathroom with the four digits 6389 set into the tiling" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lKaQv2AZ6Ps&t=185s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:05</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 12 · 3:09 · the door</span>
      <strong>Enter 6389 and the room ends</strong>
      The wooden door in the middle of the room has a black keypad: a red four-digit display on top, then yellow keys in a 1-9 grid with an X and an enter arrow underneath. Type <b>6 3 8 9</b> and press the arrow. In the recording the display holds that number in black on red, and three seconds later the door is standing ajar with the orange door icon floating beside it — the same exit marker the game uses in every room. That is the last gameplay frame before the video fades out.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l7-door-keypad-6389.jpg" alt="The black door keypad in Master Bathroom with a red display showing 6389" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lKaQv2AZ6Ps&t=189s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:09</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 13 · 3:12 · out</span>
      <strong>The exit frame</strong>
      The door is ajar, the room is behind you, and the video has fifteen seconds left — all of them fade. If you are checking your own run against this one, this is the frame to match: door open, exit icon visible, no results panel yet. Anything the game pays you for Master Bathroom happens after this shot, so it is not on this page.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l7-exit-door.jpg" alt="The wooden door of Master Bathroom standing ajar with the orange exit icon at the end of Chapter 3 Level 7" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lKaQv2AZ6Ps&t=192s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:12</a></div>
    </div>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p><b>The cistern will not take 271651.</b> It will not, and that is the point of the middle tile. The oval tile is rotatable — the curved arrow above the slot is the tell — and 16 upside down is <b>91</b>. The panel wants <b>279151</b>. If you typed the tile as you first saw it, the last four digits are wrong and nothing happens: there is no error message on this panel, the lid simply stays shut.</p>
    <p><b>You cannot find the second keypad.</b> It is on the door you walked past in the first ten seconds — the panelled wooden one in the middle of the room, with a black panel and a red display. It does not look like an exit while the level is running, because the exit icon only appears once the code is in. If you have 6389 and nowhere to type it, go back to the door and tap the panel rather than the handle.</p>
    <p><b>The digits on the shower tiles will not read.</b> They are the same colour as the tiles. Move the camera so the tiles are lit from the side rather than head-on, and look right of the handheld sprayer, not at the shower head. The four digits are <b>6 3 8 9</b> and they do not change.</p>
    <p><b>The drawer light puzzle does nothing.</b> It is a matching puzzle, not a container: you move the light pattern on the drawer fronts until it matches what the mirrors show, and the game moves you on. Tapping the mirrors themselves will not help.</p>
    <p><b>The towels will not stay in order.</b> The rack is a sequencing puzzle with a banner — "Order of the towels" — and the recording solves it in a few seconds without a readable target. If you are stuck there, the practical advice is the same as for any sequencing puzzle in this game: try the order the towels are stacked rather than the order you would hang them.</p>
    <p><b>The cube eats your taps.</b> "Touch order for the cube" means exactly that: the faces have to be touched in sequence, and a wrong touch resets it. Nothing on the cube tells you the sequence; the flattened 3x3 grid at 2:34 does. This is the one puzzle in the room we could not document properly, and we would rather say so than sell you a guess.</p>

    <h2>Where Master Bathroom sits in Chapter 3<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Chapter 3 is <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and Master Bathroom is room <b>7 of 15</b>: after Workshop (5) and Guest Bedroom (6), before Kids Playroom (8). It is the point in the chapter where the rooms stop being single-wall scenes. Guest Bedroom and Workshop both keep you in one frame; Master Bathroom gives you a vanity wall, a tub, a shower and a separate toilet alcove, and expects you to remember which end of it you left a clue at.</p>
    <p>The pattern it sets is the one the rest of the chapter uses: one lock whose code is composed from found objects (the three tiles), and one lock whose code is printed somewhere in the room and just needs spotting (the shower tiles). If you have done Dining Room in Chapter 3, you have seen the same grammar with two locks; the difference here is that the found-object lock needs a rotation, not just a reading.</p>

